---
/**
 * The privacy policy for micaforge.com and the hosted beta.
 *
 * Every factual claim about what the tracker records is checked against
 * docs/CONTRACT.md section 7 and the columns listed in docs/privacy/what-we-store.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import { IconOut } from "../components/react/icons.tsx";

const sections = [
  { id: "who", t: "1. Who we are" },
  { id: "account", t: "2. If you have an account" },
  { id: "site", t: "3. If you visit micaforge.com" },
  { id: "collected", t: "4. What the tracker records" },
  { id: "never", t: "5. What the tracker never records" },
  { id: "cookies", t: "6. Cookies and local storage" },
  { id: "machines", t: "7. Records of crawlers and other software" },
  { id: "shared", t: "8. Who else processes data" },
  { id: "kept", t: "9. How long it is kept" },
  { id: "rights", t: "10. Your rights" },
  { id: "changes", t: "11. Changes" },
  { id: "contact", t: "12. Contact" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · Micaforge"
  description="What Micaforge collects for account holders, for visitors of sites that use it, and for visitors of micaforge.com: no cookies on tracked sites, no raw IP addresses, no personal data."
>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main id="main">
    <article class="mf-wrap mf-doc">
      <header class="mf-doc__head">
        <h1 class="mf-doc__title">Privacy policy</h1>
        <p class="mf-doc__lede">
          Micaforge measures traffic, not people. This page says exactly what that means for you,
          whether you use Micaforge, visit a site that uses it, or visit micaforge.com.
        </p>
        <div class="mf-doc__meta">
          <span>Last changed <strong>8 October 2026</strong></span>
          <span>Covers micaforge.com and the hosted beta</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <nav class="mf-toc" aria-label="Contents">
        <ol>
          {sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.t}</a></li>)}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <Prose class="lg-body" as="section">
        <h2 id="who">1. Who we are</h2>
        <p>
          micaforge.com and the hosted Micaforge service are operated by{" "}
          <strong>Internet Technology Services LLC</strong> (&ldquo;we&rdquo;). For your account, we
          decide how your data is used. For the analytics data a site sends to Micaforge, the site
          owner decides, and we process it only on their behalf, as described in the{" "}
          <a href="/dpa">data processing terms</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you run Micaforge on your own server, none of your data reaches us: a self-hosted
          install makes no call to us at runtime.
        </p>

        <h2 id="account">2. If you have an account</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Your email address</strong>, to sign you in with one-time codes and to send the
            invitations, reports and alerts you ask for.
          </li>
          <li><strong>Your name</strong>, if you give one, and the names of your organisations and sites.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sign-in sessions</strong>: a digest of the session token, the browser string, and
            a salted hash of the IP address, to keep you signed in and to protect your account.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sign-in codes</strong>, stored only as a keyed digest, for ten minutes at most.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          We use this data to provide the service you signed up for. We do not sell it and we do not
          use it for advertising.
        </p>

        <h2 id="site">3. If you visit micaforge.com</h2>
        <p>
          We measure micaforge.com with Micaforge itself, so the tracker sections below describe what
          is recorded about your visit. No cookie is set on the public pages.
        </p>

        <h2 id="collected">4. What the tracker records</h2>
        <p>When you load a page that carries the Micaforge script, the following is recorded for the site owner.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>The page.</strong> The address you visited, its title, the query string and
            fragment, and which page you entered and left on.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where you came from.</strong> The referring site, and any campaign
            parameters in the link you followed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Your device, in categories.</strong> Browser and version, operating system
            and version, device type, screen and window size, language and time zone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Roughly where you are.</strong> Country, region and city, derived from your
            address and then discarded. Map coordinates are the coordinates of the city, not of
            you.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>What you did.</strong> Time on the previous page, how far you scrolled, and
            any custom events the site chose to send.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>How fast the page was.</strong> Standard web performance measurements.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A visitor identifier.</strong> Not a name and not a cookie: a one-way hash
            of a daily secret, the site, your address and your browser string. It changes every
            day and cannot be reversed. Tomorrow you are a different visitor to us.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A site owner can choose to attach their own account identifier to events for people who
          are signed in to their site, by calling <code>identify()</code>. It is off unless the
          site's code calls it, and it is the one case where these records can be tied to a person.
        </p>

        <h2 id="never">5. What the tracker never records</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No raw IP address is ever stored on a visitor's record.</strong> It is read
            when the request arrives, turned into a country, a region, a city and a network
            operator, folded into the daily hash, and dropped. There is no column for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No browser string is stored on a visitor's record.</strong> It is read to
            work out the browser, operating system and device type, and those categories are
            what is kept.
          </li>
          <li><strong>No cookie is set and none is read.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>No cross-site tracking, no advertising identifiers, no data sharing.</strong></li>
          <li><strong>No fingerprinting beyond the daily hash described above.</strong></li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="cookies">6. Cookies and local storage</h2>
        <p>
          The tracking script sets no cookies. It writes one value into your browser's local
          storage, <code>mf-opt-out</code>, and only if the site gives you an opt-out control and
          you use it. That value stays in your browser and is never sent anywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Micaforge dashboard sets one session cookie, <code>mf_session</code>, for people who
          sign in to it. It is needed to keep you signed in and is not used for anything else.
        </p>

        <h2 id="machines">7. Records of crawlers and other software</h2>
        <p>
          Micaforge also records requests from software rather than people: search crawlers, AI
          agents, link previews, monitoring tools. Those records are kept in separate tables and
          carry more detail than a person's record does, including the full client string and a
          salted hash of the source address, because none of it describes a human being.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a request of yours is misclassified as a machine, the effect is that it appears in
          a table of machine traffic rather than in the visitor statistics. It is still not
          linked to you, and no further data is collected about it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="shared">8. Who else processes data</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>netcup GmbH</strong>, Germany: the server that runs Micaforge and stores its data.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cloudflare, Inc.</strong>: DNS, content delivery and network protection for
            micaforge.com and api.micaforge.com, which means requests to them pass through
            Cloudflare's network, and delivery of the emails we send.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          There is no third-party analytics service, advertising network or data broker behind
          Micaforge. We share data only where the law requires it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="kept">9. How long it is kept</h2>
        <p>
          Account data is kept while your account exists. Sign-in sessions expire after thirty days
          without use. During the beta, analytics data is kept until the site owner deletes the site
          or their account; event rows of a deleted site are removed by the next cleanup run.
        </p>
        <p>
          The daily secret used to build visitor identifiers is never kept beyond forty-eight hours,
          which is what makes yesterday's identifier permanently unlinkable to today's.
        </p>

        <h2 id="rights">10. Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          Where the GDPR or a similar law applies to you, you have rights of access, correction,
          erasure, restriction, portability and objection. Account holders can exercise them by
          writing to us.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are a visitor of a site that uses Micaforge, there is a limit worth stating. No
          cookie is set, no address is stored, and the visitor identifier changes every day and
          cannot be reversed, so neither the site owner nor we can usually find your data to show
          or delete it. That is a result of collecting less, and under Article 11 of the GDPR no
          one is required to collect more data only to identify you. Where a site attached its own
          account identifier to events, ask that site: with the identifier, those rows can be found
          and deleted.
        </p>
        <p>You can also complain to your local data protection authority.</p>

        <h2 id="changes">11. Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If this policy changes in a way that matters, we will update the date at the top and tell
          account holders by email.
        </p>

        <h2 id="contact">12. Contact</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Internet Technology Services LLC</strong><br />
          Email: <a href="mailto:contact@micaforge.com">contact@micaforge.com</a>
        </p>
      </Prose>

      <div class="mf-next">
        <a class="mf-link" href="/docs/privacy/what-we-store">Every column, listed<IconOut size={13} /></a>
        <a class="mf-link" href="/docs/privacy/cookieless-identification">How the daily hash works<IconOut size={13} /></a>
        <a class="mf-link" href="/dpa">Data processing<IconOut size={13} /></a>
        <a class="mf-link" href="/terms">Terms<IconOut size={13} /></a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lg-body { margin-top: var(--mf-12); }
</style>
